ething else was  different we were still shouting  obscenities but  this time not at one another in private  but into a headset  at strangers over xbox live i watched as  my male  counterparts formed a tribe-like bond  with these other players  they had repeated sessions routinely  planned every week  online bromances formed over halo 3 call  of duty 4 and  gears with their gamer tags boosting a  player formed  clan name and honestly it was kind of  amazing seeing these strangers connect  in this way endearing really but it all  equally very quickly started to feel  like one  big gamer for eternity and so  the xbox bro was born and i  was not one of them now it's important  that i mention that i'm not really  judging the bro gamer i just sought  value from games in a different way  and it's also important i mentioned that  the bro gamer just didn't exist in this  bubble of  xbox in fact bro culture was the most  prevalent in  the mid to early 2000s look no further  than the american pie trilogy or van  wilder or any number of arena based  multiplayer shooters  welcome to unreal tournament everyone  terms like bromance entered the oxford  dictionary in 2001  and over the following decade bro  affiliated terms began popping up  more and more bro hug bro down bohemian  most of which found their way into  esteemed publications like the new york  times  and xbox rose alongside it writing its  highest wave of success  with the boom of the bro and that wasn't  entirely  a coincidence either xbox game designer  daniel cook wrote a blog in 2013 and  said  microsoft worked on marketing campaigns  that put  quote machoism ultra violence and chimp  boys with backwards caps  in the spotlight he even called the xbox  a pre-packaged group  identity machine ouch  okay so here's where we're at bro  culture  was a thing and xbox wanted to cultivate  that again  i'm not judging there was some good  stuff that came out of it  but what sticks out to me the most is  that i finally  understand why i couldn't find a place  in the xbox  community it's not that i was projecting  that onto xbox it's that  xbox was projecting that onto me and  i wasn't really into it so seeing cook's  blog  in a lot of ways really validated my  feelings on the whole thing  but the story continues  the xbox one stumbled out of the gate  and i had already stopped paying  attention to xbox altogether however  during the years following  something interesting began to happen i  was doomed scrolling through twitter  when i locked eyes with xbox again and  something  was different about it its hair was cut  it got into shape  and it had a charming smile on its face  and  it was wearing a double fine t-shirt  wait what the [ __ ]  wait what as would churn out while i was  looking away  microsoft had acquired one of my  favorite gaming studios  double fine left and right xbox began  making choices that made me look at the  console through an entirely different  lens  i began seeing opportunities for gamers  in a way that wasn't  front and center before the chance for a  catalog of games to find new audiences  in a way that was more accessible than  ever  and most recently encouraged me not to  play games with my frat  gamer bros but grandma it was the first  time that xbox was making bold decisions  that  somehow felt as though they were  directed right  at me gone are the days where xbox  packaged a headset with xbox live and on  with the days where me  the gamer and what i want to play and  how i play it  felt like the center of attention and  you know this isn't about  my judgment of culture or who is defined  as a bro it's about how xbox became  more than that and eventually won  someone over  who had no interest in what the brand  had to offer just a few years prior  so yeah game pass is awesome we know  that  but that service is all but a cog in the  gaps the xbox  is closing and the bridges it's creating  by making an entry point  for everyone from the casual gamer to  the hardcore  regardless of age generation or  accessibility requirements  all of which is bookended with a clear  choice to invest in creative developers  not trying to cash in on one specific  demographic but to propel gaming  as the medium it was intended to be a  space  for everyone i don't know what an xbox  bro is these days  but you know what i think i am one  yeah yeah yeah  [Music]  megatron's here to watch me do my outro  yo folks  thanks for indulging me on this journey  of  to brodem again this is just my own  personal opinion and journey and if you  utterly disagree with me that's okay you  can leave it in the comments  that's what they're for and to say nice  things if you want  i read them so i will know mean or nice  if you're new here i'm curt this is  gamespot welcome  subscribe if your heart desires and all  that jazz and notifications  you know whatever suits you best anyways  we do a bunch of xbox content we do a  bunch of video game content so  if that's a thing you like then consider  sticking around here  okay be well play your games regardless  of  what console they're on you're cool no  matter what okay  [Music]  bye  you
 

